Let $D \subset \Gamma$ be a finite set. We say that a set $L \subseteq \Gamma$ is \emphd{left $D$-separated} if $D\lambda_1 \cap D\lambda_2 = \varnothing$ for every pair of distinct $\lambda_1$, $\lambda_2 \in L$.  egin{lemma} If $D$, $F \subset \Gamma$ are finite sets, then $F$ has a left $D$-separated subset $L \subseteq F$ with \[|L| \geqslant |F|/|D|^2.\] \end{lemma}  egin{scproof} First recall some basic facts about finite graphs. Let $G$ be a finite graph with vertex set $V$ and edge set $E$. A subset $I \subseteq V$ is \emphd{independent \ep{in $G$}} if no two vertices in $I$ are adjacent to each other. A straightforward greedy construction shows that if every vertex of $G$ has at most $d$ neighbors, then $V$ can be partitioned into $d+1$ independent sets. In particular, $G$ has an independent set of size at least $|V|/(d+1)$. Now let $G$ be the graph with vertex set $F$ in which distinct vertices $\sigma$, $\sigma' \in F$ are adjacent if and only if $D\sigma \cap D\sigma' 
eq \varnothing$, or, equivalently, $\sigma' \in D^{-1}D\sigma$. Every $\sigma \in F$ has at most $|D^{-1}D| - 1 \leqslant |D|^2 - 1$ neighbors in $G$ \ep{we are subtracting $1$ to account for the fact that $\sigma$ is not adjacent to itself}. Therefore, $G$ has an independent set $L$ of size at least $|F|/|D|^2$, and that is precisely what we need. \end{scproof} For a partial map $\varphi \colon \Gamma ightharpoonup k$ with finite domain, let $V_\varphi \subseteq k^\Gamma$ denote the clopen set given by \[ V_\varphi \,\coloneqq\, \set{x \in k^\Gamma \,:\, x \supset \varphi}. \] Since the topology on $k^\Gamma$ is generated by the sets of the form $V_\varphi$, it is enough to prove Theorem~ef{theo:synd} with $V = V_\varphi$. So, let $D \subset \Gamma$ be a nonempty finite set and let $\varphi \colon D 	o k$. Let $\ell_0 \in {\mathbb{N}}$ be such that  egin{equation} e \cdot (1 - k^{-|D|})^\ell \cdot |D|^2\ell^2 \,<\, 1 \qquad 	ext{for all } \ell \geqslant \ell_0. \end{equation} \ep{Such $\ell_0$ exists since the left-hand side of \eqref{eq:endgame} approaches $0$ as $\ell$ goes to infinity.} We shall argue that $n \coloneqq \ell_0|D|^2$ is as desired. Take any finite set $F \subset \Gamma$ of size at least $n$ and let $L \subseteq F$ be a set of size at least $|F|/|D|^2 \geqslant \ell_0$ such that $L^{-1}$ is left $D$-separated \ep{such $L$ exists due to Lemma~ef{lemma:indep} applied with $F^{-1}$ in place of $F$}. Define a $k$-instance ${\mathscr{B}}$ over $\Gamma$ as follows. For each $\gamma \in \Gamma$, let $B_\gamma$ be the bad $k$-event with domain $D_\gamma \coloneqq D L^{-1}\gamma$ consisting of all maps $\psi \colon D_\gamma 	o k$ such that: \[ 	ext{for each } \lambda \in L,\ 	ext{there is } \delta \in D\ 	ext{with } \psi(\delta\lambda^{-1}\gamma) \,
eq\, \varphi(\delta). \] Notice that since $L^{-1}$ is left $D$-separated, $D_\gamma$ is equal to the \emph{disjoint} union of the sets $D \lambda^{-1}\gamma$, $\lambda \in L$. Let ${\mathscr{B}}\coloneqq \set{B_\gamma \,:\, \gamma \in \Gamma}$. We claim that if $x \colon \Gamma	o k$ is a solution to ${\mathscr{B}}$, then $x$ is trapped in $L \cdot V_\varphi$ \ep{and hence also in $F \cdot V_\varphi$}. Indeed, consider any $\gamma \in \Gamma$. Since $x$ avoids $B_\gamma$, the following holds: \[ 	ext{there is } \lambda \in L\ 	ext{such that for all } \delta \in D, \ x(\delta \lambda^{-1}\gamma) \,=\, \varphi(\delta). \] But this precisely means that for some $\lambda \in L$, $\lambda^{-1}\gamma \cdot x \supset \varphi$, i.e., $\gamma \cdot x \in L \cdot V_\varphi$. In view of Theorem~ef{theo:LLL}, it only remains to show that ${\mathscr{B}}$ is correct for the LLL. First we bound $d({\mathscr{B}})$. Consider any $\gamma \in \Gamma$. We need to find an upper bound on the size of the set \[ \set{\gamma' \in \Gamma \setminus \set{\gamma} \,:\, D_\gamma \cap D_{\gamma'} 
eq \varnothing}. \] To this end, notice that $D_\gamma \cap D_{\gamma'} 
eq \varnothing$ if and only if $\gamma' \in LD^{-1}DL^{-1}\gamma$. Thus, \[ \left|\set{\gamma' \in \Gamma \setminus \set{\gamma} \,:\, D_\gamma \cap D_{\gamma'} 
eq \varnothing}ight| \,=\, |LD^{-1}DL^{-1}| - 1 \,\leqslant\, |D|^2|L|^2 - 1, \] and hence we conclude that $d({\mathscr{B}}) \leqslant |D|^2|L|^2 - 1$. Next we bound $p({\mathscr{B}})$. Again, fix $\gamma \in \Gamma$ and draw $f \colon D_\gamma 	o k$ uniformly at random. Then for each $\lambda \in L$, \[ \mathbb{P}[f(\delta \lambda^{-1} \gamma) 
eq \varphi(\delta) 	ext{ for some } \delta \in D] \,=\, 1 - k^{-|D|}, \] and since the sets $D \lambda^{-1}\gamma$, $\lambda \in L$ are disjoint, we conclude that \[ \mathbb{P}[B_\gamma] \,=\, (1 - k^{-|D|})^{|L|}. \] Therefore, $p({\mathscr{B}}) \leqslant (1 - k^{-|D|})^{|L|}$. To put everything together, ${\mathscr{B}}$ is correct as long as \[ e \cdot (1 - k^{-|D|})^{|L|} \cdot |D|^2|L|^2 \,<\, 1. \] Since $|L| \geqslant \ell_0$, \eqref{eq:endgame} gives the desired result.
